<think>Alright, so I have this problem here about a regular 15-sided polygon, also known as a pentadecagon, right? The question is asking for the area of the annulus that's bounded by the circles inscribed in the polygon and circumscribed around it. The side length of the polygon is given as 2a. Hmm, okay, let me break this down step by step. First, I need to recall what an annulus is. If I remember correctly, an annulus is the region between two concentric circles, one inside the other. So in this case, the inner circle is the incircle of the polygon (the circle inscribed in the polygon), and the outer circle is the circumcircle of the polygon (the circle circumscribed around the polygon). The area of the annulus would then be the area of the larger circle minus the area of the smaller circle. Got that part. 

Now, to find the areas of both circles, I need their radii. The problem gives me the side length of the polygon, which is 2a. But I need to relate this side length to the radii of the incircle and the circumcircle. Hmm, how do you find the radii of the incircle
